## 2. `Copy` Types: The Exception

### Exercise 2.1 — Copy values are free to reuse

**Goal**: swap two `Copy` values without any ownership ceremony.

**Info**: `i32` moves nowhere: assignment copies, parameters copy, returns copy. Every use of a `Copy` value is independent of every other.

**Objectives**

- return the two values in swapped order
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §2.1 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: a one-tuple tail expression.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return `(b, a)`.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the swapped pair.
fn swap_copy(a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, i32) {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
assert_eq!(swap_copy(1, 2), (2, 1));
assert_eq!(swap_copy(-3, 0), (0, -3));
assert_eq!(swap_copy(7, 7), (7, 7));
println!("Exercise 2.1 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 2.2 — One non-`Copy` field changes the whole contract

**Goal**: read a `Vec` of owned pairs without moving any of them.

**Info**: `(String, u32)` is not `Copy` — the `String` forbids it. Elements cannot be moved out of a shared borrow, so "find the biggest and return it" needs either a clone or a restructure. Here the clone is the deliberate, visible cost.

**Objectives**

- return the `(symbol, ticks)` pair with the highest `ticks`, as an owned tuple
- return `None` for an empty slice
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §2.2 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: `iter().max_by_key(...)` yields a reference to the winner; `.map` plus `.clone()` turns it into an owned pair.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return the pair with the highest `ticks`, owned; `None` when `prices` is empty.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the search-and-clone.
fn biggest_tick(prices: &[(String, u32)]) -> Option<(String, u32)> {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let quotes = vec![(String::from("A"), 3u32), (String::from("B"), 7u32)];
assert_eq!(biggest_tick(&quotes), Some((String::from("B"), 7)));
assert_eq!(biggest_tick(&[]), None);
let single = vec![(String::from("C"), 1u32)];
assert_eq!(biggest_tick(&single), Some((String::from("C"), 1)));
assert_eq!(quotes.len(), 2);
println!("Exercise 2.2 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 2.3 — A tuple of `Copy` elements is `Copy`

**Goal**: use one small value several times over, the way only `Copy` types allow.

**Info**: `(i32, i32)` is `Copy` because both elements are. The tuple can be destructured, passed, and read again — no move ever happens, so nothing is ever "gone".

**Objectives**

- return `(x + y, x - y, x * y)` for the pair
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §2.3 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: destructure once with `let (x, y) = point;`, then use both freely.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
