## 3. Borrowing: `&T` and `&mut T`

### Exercise 3.1 — Many shared borrows at once

**Goal**: read several values through shared borrows in one pass.

**Info**: A `&[&str]` parameter is one borrow of the slice, whose elements are themselves shared borrows. Nothing here mutates, so nothing here conflicts.

**Objectives**

- return the summed byte length of all the strings
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §3.1 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: `iter().map(...).sum()` over the slice.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return the summed byte length of all the strings.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the sum.
fn total_len(texts: &[&str]) -> usize {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
assert_eq!(total_len(&["a", "bb"]), 3);
assert_eq!(total_len(&[]), 0);
assert_eq!(total_len(&["é"]), 2);
assert_eq!(total_len(&["", "", ""]), 0);
println!("Exercise 3.1 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 3.2 — One mutable borrow does the writing

**Goal**: change the caller's value in place through `&mut String`.

**Info**: The `&mut` borrow is exclusive while it lives — which is exactly why the caller can see the change afterwards: there was never a second copy.

**Objectives**

- append `!` to `text` in place
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §3.2 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: `push` on the dereferenced binding is just `text.push(...)` — auto-deref handles it.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Append `!` to `text` in place.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the in-place mutation.
fn append_bang(text: &mut String) {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let mut first = String::from("hey");
append_bang(&mut first);
assert_eq!(first, "hey!");
let mut second = String::new();
append_bang(&mut second);
assert_eq!(second, "!");
println!("Exercise 3.2 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 3.3 — Accept everything string-like

**Goal**: write the parameter type that makes every call site work.

**Info**: A `&str` parameter accepts a literal, a `&String` (deref coercion) and a `String` slice, with no conversion anywhere. This is the convention from chapter 04, now explicable: coercion goes through `Deref`, and a borrow costs nothing.

**Objectives**

- return the byte length of `text`
- keep the signature unchanged — the point *is* the signature

**See**: §3.3 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: the body is one method call; the exercises are the four call sites below.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return the byte length of `text`.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the length.
fn text_len(text: &str) -> usize {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let owned = String::from("deref");
assert_eq!(text_len(&owned), 5);
assert_eq!(text_len("literal"), 7);
assert_eq!(text_len(&owned[0..2]), 2);
assert_eq!(text_len(""), 0);
