**Goal**: return an `Option<&T>` and know it costs nothing at runtime.

**Info**: The niche optimisation makes `Option<&T>` the same size as `&T`, so "maybe a reference" is not a cost you are paying for — it is the standard way to say "the value may not be there" without copying the payload.

**Objectives**

- return `Some(&first)` when `needle` is found in `items`, `None` otherwise
- the reference must point into the original slice, not a copy
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §11.5 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: `iter().find(...)` already yields `Option<&&T>`; one `.copied()` flattens it.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return a reference to the first element equal to `needle`.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the borrowed search.
fn find_ref<'a>(items: &'a [String], needle: &str) -> Option<&'a String> {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let items = vec![String::from("a"), String::from("b")];
assert_eq!(find_ref(&items, "b"), Some(&String::from("b")));
assert_eq!(find_ref(&items, "z"), None);
assert_eq!(find_ref(&[], "a"), None);
assert_eq!(items.len(), 2);
println!("Exercise 11.5 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
