## 12. The Borrow Checker in Real Loops

### Exercise 12.1 — Iterate by reference

**Goal**: read every element of an owned `Vec` and keep the `Vec`.

**Info**: `for x in &items` is the spelled-out `items.iter()`. Either form borrows; the plain `for x in items` would have moved the whole vector into the loop.

**Objectives**

- return the summed byte length of all the strings, leaving `items` owned and intact
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §12.1 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: the loop is `for item in items` — the function's *parameter* is already a borrow, so no conflict; the discipline is that `items` stays usable by the caller afterwards.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return the summed byte length of the strings, borrowing throughout.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the borrow-and-sum.
fn sum_lengths(items: &[String]) -> usize {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let owned = vec![String::from("ab"), String::from("c")];
assert_eq!(sum_lengths(&owned), 3);
assert_eq!(owned.len(), 2);
assert_eq!(sum_lengths(&[]), 0);
println!("Exercise 12.1 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 12.2 — Collect, then extend

**Goal**: grow a collection while reading it, without fighting the borrow checker.

**Info**: The direct approach — `push` inside `for x in &items` — is an `E0502`: the loop holds the shared borrow for its whole body. The fix is structural: collect the additions first, then extend.

**Objectives**

- return a *new* `Vec` containing every element of `items` followed by `"!"` appended versions of every element shorter than 3 bytes
- `items` must be unchanged
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §12.3 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: one loop over `&items` collecting into `additions`, then one `extend` — the same shape as §12.3 of the example notebook.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return `items` plus `!`-suffixed copies of every element shorter than 3 bytes.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the collect-then-extend pattern.
fn with_short_shouted(items: &[String]) -> Vec<String> {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let base = vec![String::from("ab"), String::from("long"), String::from("x")];
assert_eq!(
    with_short_shouted(&base),
    vec!["ab", "long", "x", "ab!", "x!"]
);
assert_eq!(base.len(), 3);
assert_eq!(with_short_shouted(&[]), Vec::<String>::new());
println!("Exercise 12.2 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 12.3 — `retain` instead of remove-while-iterating

**Goal**: prune a map in place without an `E0502`.

**Info**: `retain` keeps only the entries whose closure returns `true`. The mutation happens inside the map's own borrow, which is why the closure can look at keys and values while the map is being modified.

**Objectives**

- remove every `(key, value)` entry of `scores` whose value is zero, in place
- return how many entries were removed
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §12.4 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: capture the original `len()` before `retain`; the difference is the answer.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Remove every zero-score entry of `scores` in place; return how many were removed.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with the retain.
fn drop_zero_scores(scores: &mut std::collections::HashMap<String, i32>) -> usize {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let mut scores: std::collections::HashMap<String, i32> =
    [("a", 0), ("b", 3), ("c", 0)]
        .into_iter()
        .map(|(key, value)| (key.to_string(), value))
        .collect();
assert_eq!(drop_zero_scores(&mut scores), 2);
assert_eq!(scores.len(), 1);
assert!(scores.contains_key("b"));
assert_eq!(drop_zero_scores(&mut scores), 0);
println!("Exercise 12.3 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
## 13. Reading the Compiler: The Five Codes

### Exercise 13.1 — Name the code

**Goal**: connect each broken snippet to the diagnostic it produces.

**Info**: The snippets are comments. Matching the failure to its code is half of reading the compiler; the other half is knowing the fix. No `todo!()` here — the body is a string, and the check block compares it.

**Objectives**

- fill in the `code_for` match arms with the right five codes, as `"E0382"`, `"E0502"`, `"E0507"`, `"E0499"` and `"E0106"`
- keep the match shape unchanged

**See**: §13 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: `let s2 = s1; println!("{s1}")` is `E0382`; two inputs, one borrowed output is `E0106`.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Return the diagnostic code each broken snippet produces.
///
/// TODO: replace every `todo!()` with the correct code string.
fn code_for(case: usize) -> &'static str {
    // case 0: let s2 = s1; println!("{s1}")        — use after move
    // case 1: let r = &x; let m = &mut x; read r   — shared + mutable overlap
    // case 2: let r = &owned; let v = *r;          — move out of a shared borrow
    // case 3: let a = &mut x; let b = &mut x;      — two live mutable borrows
    // case 4: fn f(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str       — elision cannot decide
    match case {
        0 => todo!(),
        1 => todo!(),
        2 => todo!(),
        3 => todo!(),
        4 => todo!(),
        _ => "unknown",
    }
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
assert_eq!(code_for(0), "E0382");
assert_eq!(code_for(1), "E0502");
assert_eq!(code_for(2), "E0507");
assert_eq!(code_for(3), "E0499");
assert_eq!(code_for(4), "E0106");
assert_eq!(code_for(9), "unknown");
println!("Exercise 13.1 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 13.2 — Apply the fixes

**Goal**: convert three failing patterns into their compiling equivalents, in one function.

**Info**: Each sub-case in the check block mirrors one diagnostic from §13: the clone fix for `E0382`, the read-reorder for `E0502`, and the read-don't-move fix for `E0507`. Every one of them is a two-line pattern.

**Objectives**

- implement all three helper functions so their check blocks pass
- `clone_fix` must not consume `text` — the caller uses it afterwards
- `reorder_fix` must read first, then push — the checks observe both
- `read_fix` must not move out of the borrow
- keep the signatures unchanged

**See**: §13.1 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: `clone_fix` is `.clone()`; `reorder_fix` reads both chars *before* `push`; `read_fix` returns `text.len()`.
</VSCode.Cell>
