## 15. Mental Model and Habits

§15 of the example notebook is habits, not demonstrations — there is no code cell to mirror. Two of its habits, though, are worth turning into exercises here, because they are checkable:

- **Say the two-line story** (§13.1 above tests exactly that).
- **Shrink the problem**: when a program will not compile, comment the failing line out, get the rest green, and reintroduce it.

The next section is the mixed practice; treat it as the habit test.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
## 16. Review: Mixed Problems

### Exercise 16.1 — A value that changes hands three times

**Goal**: route one value through consume, mutate, and borrow — in the right order.

**Info**: The order is forced: anything that consumes must run last, anything that borrows mutably must not overlap anything else. `swap_case` is given a correct body; the exercise is `pipeline`, where the calls have to be sequenced so the compiler accepts all three.

